19
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:26.153
Y2K. Maybe the most famous bug.

20
00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:29.953
Back in the sixties, memory was expensive.

21
00:02:30.213 --> 00:02:32.413
So programmers saved space:

22
00:02:32.413 --> 00:02:35.633
they stored the year with just two digits.

23
00:02:36.413 --> 00:02:38.793
1985? Became “85.”

24
00:02:39.073 --> 00:02:42.553
Then comes the year 2000: “double-zero.”

25
00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:45.553
And the computer reads it as… 1900.

26
00:02:45.773 --> 00:02:49.093
Born in ’85? Zero minus 85:

27
00:02:49.293 --> 00:02:51.593
you’re negative 85 years old.

28
00:02:51.593 --> 00:02:54.873
Nobody thought this code would still run in 2000.

29
00:02:54.953 --> 00:02:56.053
It did.

30
00:02:56.193 --> 00:02:59.233
And the two-digit year wasn’t hidden in one module.

31
00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:01.913
It was copied into every calculation.

32
00:03:02.073 --> 00:03:04.453
So engineers spent years hunting down

33
00:03:04.453 --> 00:03:06.773
every line that touched a year.

34
00:03:06.773 --> 00:03:09.813
Midnight came… and almost nothing broke,

35
00:03:09.813 --> 00:03:12.053
because they fixed it in time.

36
00:03:12.313 --> 00:03:14.753
The bill? Billions.

37
00:03:15.073 --> 00:03:19.813
Lesson one: every module should keep a secret.

38
00:03:19.813 --> 00:03:31.500
